    "claims": [
      {
        "claim_id": "c1",
        "status": "introduced",
        "necessary": "yes",
        "before": {
          "requirement": "Initialize notes before the target-called pass creates sections.",
          "applicability": "required",
          "harness_status": "sat",
          "reason": "The fixed entry calls read_begin, whose before version initializes notes before the entry calls perform_an_assembly_pass.",
          "source_evidence": [
            {
              "evidence_id": "loc-f48e93544875002be2da1799",
              "quote": "void\nread_begin (void)\n{\n  const char *p;\n\n  pobegin ();\n  obj_read_begin_hook ();\n\n  /* Something close -- but not too close -- to a multiple of 1024.\n     The debugging malloc I'm using has 24 bytes of overhead.  */\n  obstack_begin (&notes, chunksize);\n  obstack_begin (&cond_obstack, chunksize);\n"
            },
            {
              "evidence_id": "loc-4dd24ac87ca82b114dac2d91",
              "quote": "#ifndef OBJ_MACH_O\n  /* Create the standard sections, and those the assembler uses\n     internally.  */\n  text_section = subseg_new (TEXT_SECTION_NAME, 0);\n  data_section = subseg_new (DATA_SECTION_NAME, 0);\n  bss_section = subseg_new (BSS_SECTION_NAME, 0);\n"
            },
            {
              "evidence_id": "loc-191469848d34737f336653e7",
              "quote": "static void\nalloc_seginfo (segT seg)\n{\n  segment_info_type *seginfo;\n\n  seginfo = obstack_alloc (&notes, sizeof (*seginfo));\n  memset (seginfo, 0, sizeof (*seginfo));\n  seginfo->bfd_section = seg;\n  bfd_set_section_userdata (seg, seginfo);\n"
            }
          ]
        },
        "after": {
          "requirement": "Initialize notes before the target-called pass creates sections.",
          "applicability": "required",
          "harness_status": "unsat",
          "reason": "After the change, read_begin does not initialize notes. Initialization occurs in main, which the fixed entry bypasses, while the pass still allocates section metadata from notes.",
          "source_evidence": [
            {
              "evidence_id": "loc-9b31aeedfa355ace26dfb3c7",
              "quote": "void\nread_begin (void)\n{\n  const char *p;\n\n  pobegin ();\n  obj_read_begin_hook ();\n\n  obstack_begin (&cond_obstack, chunksize);\n"
            },
            {
              "evidence_id": "loc-bb75f5ac82c40a82dff001aa",
              "quote": "  obstack_begin (&notes, chunksize);\n  xatexit (free_notes);\n\n  PROGRESS (1);\n  /* Call parse_args before any of the init/begin functions\n     so that switches like --hash-size can be honored.  */\n  parse_args (&argc, &argv);\n"
            },
            {
              "evidence_id": "loc-b95f55422c863b14afce0925",
              "quote": "#ifndef OBJ_MACH_O\n  /* Create the standard sections, and those the assembler uses\n     internally.  */\n  text_section = subseg_new (TEXT_SECTION_NAME, 0);\n  data_section = subseg_new (DATA_SECTION_NAME, 0);\n  bss_section = subseg_new (BSS_SECTION_NAME, 0);\n"
            },
            {
              "evidence_id": "loc-bb86a6bf3d0d15cc53b8c20e",
              "quote": "static void\nalloc_seginfo (segT seg)\n{\n  segment_info_type *seginfo;\n\n  seginfo = obstack_alloc (&notes, sizeof (*seginfo));\n  memset (seginfo, 0, sizeof (*seginfo));\n  seginfo->bfd_section = seg;\n  bfd_set_section_userdata (seg, seginfo);\n"
            }
          ]
        },
        "same_blocker": "no",
        "reason": "The target call sequence is fixed. Before, its read_begin call initializes notes; after, that initialization is moved to bypassed main. The section creation and allocation route is unchanged.",
